@node FORTRAN READ Statement
@section READ Statement

The READ statement is used to input data from standard input, files, or formatted I/O streams. It can read numeric values, character strings, and structured records with precise format specifications.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
READ(unit, format, advance='NO' | 'YES', iostat=iostat-var, size=size-var) &
     [input-list]

READ *, input-list
READ(unit, *, iostat=iostat-var) input-list
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{unit}: Logical unit number (*, keyboard=5, file=20+)
@item @var{format}: Format specification (FORMATted label or parenthesized spec)
@item @var{advance}: 'NO' for non-advancing, 'YES' for advancing (default)
@item @var{iostat}: Error status variable (integer)
@item @var{size}: Number of characters read (non-advancing only)
@item @var{input-list}: Variables to receive data
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM ReadBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 binary_int
    REAL*8 binary_real
    
    ! Example 1: Simple integer input
    READ *, binary_int
    PRINT *, 'Read integer:', binary_int
    
    ! Example 2: Formatted binary reading with iostat
    INTEGER*4 iostat
    READ(5, '(B32)', IOSTAT=iostat) binary_int
    IF (iostat == 0) THEN
        PRINT *, 'Binary value:', binary_int
    END IF
    
    ! Example 3: Double precision with binary specification
    READ(5, '(Z16)') binary_real
    PRINT *, 'Real binary:', binary_real
    
END PROGRAM ReadBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM ReadDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 dec_int
    REAL*8 dec_real
    CHARACTER*20 dec_string
    
    ! Example 1: Decimal integer reading
    READ *, dec_int
    PRINT *, 'Integer:', dec_int
    
    ! Example 2: Decimal real with format specification
    READ(5, '(F10.2)') dec_real
    PRINT *, 'Decimal real:', dec_real
    
    ! Example 3: String and integer combination
    READ(5, '(A20, I5)') dec_string, dec_int
    PRINT *, TRIM(dec_string), dec_int
    
END PROGRAM ReadDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM ReadDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*40 display_string
    INTEGER*4 display_int
    REAL*8 display_real
    
    ! Example 1: Display formatted string
    READ(5, '(A40)') display_string
    PRINT *, 'Display string:', TRIM(display_string)
    
    ! Example 2: Non-advancing read with display format
    READ(5, '(I5)', ADVANCE='NO', SIZE=display_int) display_int
    
    ! Example 3: Mixed display format
    READ(5, '(G15.8)') display_real
    PRINT *, 'G-format real:', display_real
    
END PROGRAM ReadDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Edge Cases and Advanced Features

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{EOF handling}: iostat returns -1 on end-of-file, critical for proper I/O loops
@item @strong{Numeric overflow}: Reading values exceeding type limits causes runtime error or unpredictable truncation
@item @strong{Format mismatch}: Non-conforming input data causes runtime error unless iostat present for error handling
@item @strong{Precision loss}: Reading high-precision decimals into REAL*4 causes silent truncation of significant digits
@item @strong{String overflow}: String input exceeding declared length is truncated silently in fixed-format mode
@item @strong{Non-advancing I/O}: SIZE parameter captures actual characters read when ADVANCE='NO'
@item @strong{Record boundaries}: Non-advancing operations may span multiple records in unformatted mode
@item @strong{List-directed format}: Asterisk (*) format follows processor-dependent formatting rules
@item @strong{Assumed-size arrays}: READ with arrays requires proper index specifications
@item @strong{INQUIRE integration}: Can check unit status before READ to prevent errors
@end itemize

@subsection Advanced Usage Patterns

The READ statement with IOSTAT provides robust error handling essential for production code. Combining iostat with conditional blocks enables graceful degradation and meaningful error messages to users. For high-performance I/O operations, consider buffering strategies and appropriate format specifications to minimize conversion overhead.

When reading BINARY data, ensure format specifiers (B, Z) match machine precision and endianness. DECIMAL formats (F, E, G) require careful specification of decimal places to avoid both information loss and excessive precision requests that waste memory and CPU cycles. DISPLAY formats (A) work with any character data but require explicit length specification for reliable output.

@node FORTRAN WRITE Statement
@section WRITE Statement

The WRITE statement outputs data to standard output, files, or formatted streams. It provides complete control over numeric representation and formatting with configurable precision and width.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
WRITE(unit, format, advance='NO' | 'YES', iostat=iostat-var) &
      [output-list]

WRITE(*, format) output-list
WRITE(unit, '(...)') output-list
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{unit}: Logical unit number (6=stdout, 20+=files)
@item @var{format}: Format label or parenthesized specification
@item @var{advance}: Controls record advancement
@item @var{iostat}: Error status variable
@item @var{output-list}: Expressions to output
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM WriteBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 bin_val = 42
    REAL*8 bin_real = 3.141592653589793D0
    
    ! Example 1: Binary integer output
    WRITE(*, '(B32)') bin_val
    
    ! Example 2: Hexadecimal representation (binary format)
    WRITE(6, '(Z16)') bin_val
    
    ! Example 3: Multiple binary values
    WRITE(*, '(B8, 1X, B16)') IAND(bin_val, 15), bin_val
    
END PROGRAM WriteBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM WriteDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 dec_val = 12345
    REAL*8 dec_real = 987.654321D0
    CHARACTER*10 dec_str = "DecimalOut"
    
    ! Example 1: Fixed decimal format
    WRITE(*, '(I10, F12.2)') dec_val, dec_real
    
    ! Example 2: Exponential decimal notation
    WRITE(6, '(E15.7)') dec_real
    
    ! Example 3: General format (selects F or E automatically)
    WRITE(*, '(G20.10)') dec_real
    
END PROGRAM WriteDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM WriteDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*30 display_msg = "Display Output Message"
    INTEGER*4 display_count = 5
    REAL*8 display_val = 99.9999D0
    
    ! Example 1: Display formatted character string
    WRITE(*, '(A30)') display_msg
    
    ! Example 2: Right-justified display output
    WRITE(6, '(I10, 2X, F10.3)') display_count, display_val
    
    ! Example 3: Non-advancing display output
    WRITE(*, '(A)', ADVANCE='NO') 'Status: '
    WRITE(*, '(I5)') display_count
    
END PROGRAM WriteDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Edge Cases and Output Management

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Format overflow}: Output exceeding field width causes asterisks (*) to display instead of value
@item @strong{Precision loss}: High-precision REAL*8 values truncated by format specification like F6.2
@item @strong{Numeric conversion}: Implicit type conversions from REAL to INTEGER may lose fractional parts
@item @strong{Record length}: Maximum output line length is implementation-dependent (often 132 or 80 characters)
@item @strong{Non-advancing I/O}: Requires careful management of cursor position and newline handling
@item @strong{Format reusability}: Same format can be used for multiple WRITE statements efficiently
@item @strong{Scale factors}: Format specification with P allows adjustment of decimal point position
@item @strong{Blank interpretation}: Leading zeros can be suppressed or forced with format modifiers
@item @strong{Binary to display}: Z format shows hexadecimal representation, useful for low-level debugging
@item @strong{Array output}: Multi-dimensional arrays output in Fortran order (first index varies fastest)
@end itemize

@subsection Performance and Format Optimization

The WRITE statement's performance depends heavily on format specification complexity and I/O buffering. Fixed-format writes (F, I) generally perform better than dynamic formats (G, E). For large output operations, batch multiple WRITE statements to reduce context switches. Non-advancing output (ADVANCE='NO') is useful for creating columnar output or progress indicators without newlines.

@node FORTRAN PRINT Statement
@section PRINT Statement

The PRINT statement is a simplified output statement that writes to standard output (unit 6). It cannot specify file units but provides simpler syntax for screen output with optional format specifications.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
PRINT format, output-list
PRINT *, output-list
PRINT '(...)', output-list
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{format}: Format label, parenthesized spec, or * for list-directed
@item @var{output-list}: Values to print
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM PrintBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 b1 = 255, b2 = 128
    
    ! Example 1: Simple binary print
    PRINT '(B16)', b1
    
    ! Example 2: Multiple binary values with spacing
    PRINT '(B8, 1X, B8)', b1, b2
    
    ! Example 3: Binary labels
    PRINT *, 'Binary values:', b1, b2
    
END PROGRAM PrintBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM PrintDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 d1 = 1000
    REAL*8 d2 = 3.14159D0
    
    ! Example 1: Simple decimal print
    PRINT *, d1, d2
    
    ! Example 2: Formatted decimal output
    PRINT '(I5, F8.4)', d1, d2
    
    ! Example 3: Decimal with labels
    PRINT *, 'Integer:', d1, 'Real:', d2
    
END PROGRAM PrintDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM PrintDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*25 msg = "Program Output Display"
    INTEGER*4 count = 42
    
    ! Example 1: Display string with formatting
    PRINT '(A)', msg
    
    ! Example 2: Display with numeric value
    PRINT '(A, I3)', 'Count: ', count
    
    ! Example 3: List-directed display output
    PRINT *, msg, count
    
END PROGRAM PrintDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Limitations and Best Practices

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{No unit specification}: PRINT always writes to stdout (unit 6), cannot redirect
@item @strong{Format errors}: Invalid format specifications cause runtime errors
@item @strong{Output field width}: Exceeding width displays asterisks instead of values
@item @strong{Newline automatic}: PRINT always terminates with newline, use WRITE for control
@item @strong{Performance}: PRINT is generally slightly slower than WRITE due to implicit unit lookup
@item @strong{Debugging}: PRINT is convenient for quick diagnostics and temporary debugging output
@item @strong{Portability}: PRINT behavior is more portable than unit-based WRITE operations
@end itemize

@subsection Common Usage Scenarios

PRINT is ideal for debugging output, status messages, and interactive programs where simplicity matters more than performance. For serious I/O operations requiring file output or error handling, use WRITE instead. PRINT with list-directed format (PRINT *, values) provides quick output without format specification overhead.

@node FORTRAN ASSIGNMENT Statement
@section ASSIGNMENT Statement

The ASSIGNMENT statement assigns values to variables, with automatic type conversion. It supports complex expressions, array element assignment, and substring operations with full numeric type support.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
variable = expression
array-element = expression
substring = expression
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{variable}: Scalar or array element target
@item @var{expression}: Any valid FORTRAN expression
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM AssignmentBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 b1, b2, b3
    INTEGER*2 b4
    
    ! Example 1: Simple binary assignment
    b1 = 42
    
    ! Example 2: Binary arithmetic operations
    b2 = b1 + 8
    b3 = IBITS(b1, 2, 4)
    
    ! Example 3: Type conversion in assignment
    b4 = INT2(b1 / 2)
    
    PRINT *, b1, b2, b3, b4
END PROGRAM AssignmentBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM AssignmentDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    REAL*8 d1, d2, d3
    INTEGER*4 d4
    
    ! Example 1: Decimal assignment
    d1 = 3.14159265359D0
    
    ! Example 2: Decimal expression
    d2 = d1 * 2.0D0 + 1.5D0
    
    ! Example 3: Mixed type assignment
    d3 = DBLE(42) / 3.0D0
    d4 = INT(d3)
    
    PRINT *, d1, d2, d3, d4
END PROGRAM AssignmentDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM AssignmentDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*30 str1, str2, str3
    CHARACTER*10 substring
    
    ! Example 1: Character string assignment
    str1 = "Display Assignment Example"
    
    ! Example 2: String concatenation
    str2 = TRIM(str1) // " - Continued"
    
    ! Example 3: Substring assignment
    substring = str1(1:10)
    
    PRINT *, TRIM(str1)
    PRINT *, TRIM(str2)
    PRINT *, TRIM(substring)
END PROGRAM AssignmentDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Type Safety and Conversion Behavior

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Type mismatch}: Automatic conversion may lose precision (REAL to INTEGER truncates fractional part)
@item @strong{Array bounds}: Assignment outside array bounds causes memory corruption and undefined behavior
@item @strong{String truncation}: Right-hand side truncated if longer than target (silent with no warning)
@item @strong{String extension}: Right-hand side padded with spaces if shorter than target
@item @strong{Numeric overflow}: Exceeding type range causes undefined behavior or wrapped values
@item @strong{Logical to numeric}: Converting LOGICAL to numeric produces -1 for .TRUE., 0 for .FALSE.
@item @strong{Complex to real}: Imaginary component silently discarded in conversion
@item @strong{Array assignment}: Entire array can be assigned if types and shapes match
@item @strong{Derived types}: Custom-defined types may have assignment rules via operator overloading
@item @strong{Expression evaluation}: Right-hand side fully evaluated before assignment starts
@item @strong{Side effects}: Functions in expressions have side effects evaluated during assignment
@end itemize

@subsection Optimization Considerations

Assignment operations are fundamental to performance. Repeated assignments in loops create pressure on memory subsystems and cache. Vectorization opportunities arise when assigning array sections. Modern Fortran compilers optimize simple scalar assignments to negligible overhead. Avoid assignments in inner loop conditions where possible, performing computation outside loops instead.

@node FORTRAN IF Single-Line
@section IF Single-Line Statement

The single-line IF statement conditionally executes one statement without THEN keyword. Ideal for simple conditional operations with minimal overhead and clear intent.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
IF (logical-expression) statement
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{logical-expression}: Boolean condition evaluation
@item @var{statement}: Single executable statement
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM IfBinarySingle
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 b1 = 15, b2 = 7
    
    ! Example 1: Simple bit test
    IF (IAND(b1, 8) /= 0) PRINT *, 'Bit 3 set'
    
    ! Example 2: Bitwise comparison
    IF (b1 > b2) b1 = b1 - b2
    
    ! Example 3: Conditional assignment
    IF (b1 == 0) b2 = 1
    
    PRINT *, b1, b2
END PROGRAM IfBinarySingle
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM IfDecimalSingle
    IMPLICIT NONE
    REAL*8 d1 = 5.5D0, d2 = 2.0D0
    INTEGER*4 d3 = 0
    
    ! Example 1: Simple comparison
    IF (d1 > d2) PRINT *, 'd1 is larger'
    
    ! Example 2: Floating-point equality (dangerous)
    IF (ABS(d1 - 5.5D0) < 1.0D-10) d3 = 1
    
    ! Example 3: Conditional expression
    IF (d1 / d2 > 2.0D0) PRINT *, 'Ratio exceeds 2'
    
END PROGRAM IfDecimalSingle
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM IfDisplaySingle
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*20 status = "COMPLETE"
    INTEGER*4 result = 0
    
    ! Example 1: String comparison
    IF (status == "COMPLETE") result = 1
    
    ! Example 2: String length check
    IF (LEN_TRIM(status) > 5) PRINT *, 'Long status'
    
    ! Example 3: Conditional output
    IF (result == 1) PRINT *, 'Status is complete'
    
END PROGRAM IfDisplaySingle
@end smallexample

@subsection Semantic and Readability Concerns

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Complex conditions}: Not easily readable with single-line IF, difficult to debug
@item @strong{Type coercion}: Numeric comparisons with implicit conversions may produce unexpected results
@item @strong{Floating-point precision}: Equality tests problematic with REAL values due to rounding
@item @strong{Single statement only}: Cannot have multiple statements without explicit continuation
@item @strong{No else clause}: Cannot provide alternative action for false condition
@item @strong{Logical operators}: Mixing AND, OR, NOT requires careful precedence understanding
@item @strong{String comparisons}: Case-sensitive, lexicographic comparison may surprise users
@item @strong{Bitwise operations}: IAND, IOR, IEOR results depend on machine integer representation
@end itemize

@subsection When to Use Single-Line IF

Single-line IF suits simple, obvious conditions: range checks, validity tests, and assignment conditionals. Avoid for complex logic where readability suffers. Consider block IF when conditions span multiple lines or require multiple actions. Document non-obvious conditions with comments.

@node FORTRAN IF Block
@section IF/THEN/ENDIF Statement

The IF/THEN/ENDIF block structure conditionally executes multiple statements. More readable than single-line IF for complex logic, with support for nested blocks and clear control flow.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
IF (logical-expression) THEN
    statements
END IF

[LABEL:] IF (condition) THEN
    statements
END IF [LABEL]
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{logical-expression}: Boolean condition
@item @var{statements}: Multiple executable statements
@item @var{LABEL}: Optional construct label (optional)
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM IfBlockBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 flags = 12, mode = 3, result = 0
    
    ! Example 1: Multiple statements in block
    IF (IAND(flags, 4) /= 0) THEN
        result = result + 1
        PRINT *, 'Bit 2 detected'
    END IF
    
    ! Example 2: Nested IF blocks
    binary_check: IF (flags > 0) THEN
        IF (IAND(flags, 1) /= 0) THEN
            result = 10
        END IF
    END IF binary_check
    
    ! Example 3: Complex bitwise condition
    IF (IOR(flags, mode) == 15) THEN
        result = result * 2
        PRINT *, 'Combined bits match'
    END IF
    
    PRINT *, 'Result:', result
END PROGRAM IfBlockBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM IfBlockDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    REAL*8 value = 25.5D0, threshold = 20.0D0
    INTEGER*4 category = 0
    
    ! Example 1: Range checking block
    IF (value >= threshold) THEN
        category = 1
        PRINT *, 'Value meets threshold'
    END IF
    
    ! Example 2: Mathematical property checks
    IF (MOD(INT(value), 2) == 0) THEN
        PRINT *, 'Integer part is even'
        category = category + 10
    END IF
    
    ! Example 3: Precision-aware comparison
    numeric_block: IF (ABS(value - 25.5D0) < 1.0D-6) THEN
        PRINT *, 'Value approximately 25.5'
    END IF numeric_block
    
    PRINT *, 'Category:', category
END PROGRAM IfBlockDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM IfBlockDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*15 status = "PROCESSING"
    CHARACTER*30 message = ""
    INTEGER*4 code = 0
    
    ! Example 1: String-based conditional block
    IF (status == "PROCESSING") THEN
        message = "Operation in progress"
        code = 1
    END IF
    
    ! Example 2: Multiple display operations
    string_check: IF (LEN_TRIM(status) > 8) THEN
        PRINT *, 'Status has good length'
        message = TRIM(message) // " - status OK"
    END IF string_check
    
    ! Example 3: Nested string conditions
    IF (INDEX(status, "ING") > 0) THEN
        PRINT *, 'Status contains ING'
    END IF
    
    PRINT *, TRIM(message), code
END PROGRAM IfBlockDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Control Flow and Nesting Complexity

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Deep nesting}: Multiple nested blocks reduce readability and increase bug potential
@item @strong{Label scoping}: Labels must be unique within entire program scope in fixed-form Fortran
@item @strong{Unreachable code}: Dead code after unconditional statements never executes
@item @strong{Construct nesting}: Named constructs simplify multi-level EXIT statements
@item @strong{Block end labels}: Optional but highly recommended for clarity in deeply nested code
@item @strong{Condition evaluation}: Conditions fully evaluated at block entry, not re-evaluated
@item @strong{Statement scope}: Variables declared in block are visible only within that block
@item @strong{Performance impact}: Deeply nested conditions may affect branch prediction in modern CPUs
@end itemize

@subsection Structured Programming with IF Blocks

IF/THEN/ENDIF blocks enforce structured programming discipline avoiding spaghetti code. Clear block boundaries with labels improve maintainability. Each block should have single entry point (at IF) and single exit point (at ENDIF). Avoid jumping into or out of blocks with GOTO statements.

@node FORTRAN IF-THEN-ELSE
@section IF/THEN/ELSE/ENDIF Statement

The IF/THEN/ELSE/ENDIF construct provides two-way branching, with optional ELSE IF chains for multi-way decisions. Enables clear program logic with explicit alternative execution paths.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
IF (condition) THEN
    statements
[ELSE IF (condition) THEN
    statements]
[ELSE
    statements]
END IF
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{condition}: Logical expression evaluated in sequence
@item @var{statements}: Executable statements for each branch
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM IfElseBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 flags = 5, status = 0
    
    ! Example 1: Two-way binary branch
    IF (IAND(flags, 1) /= 0) THEN
        status = 1
        PRINT *, 'Bit 0 set'
    ELSE
        status = 0
        PRINT *, 'Bit 0 clear'
    END IF
    
    ! Example 2: Multi-way binary decision
    select_mode: IF (flags == 3) THEN
        status = 100
        PRINT *, 'Mode A: flags=3'
    ELSE IF (flags == 5) THEN
        status = 200
        PRINT *, 'Mode B: flags=5'
    ELSE
        status = 0
        PRINT *, 'Unknown mode'
    END IF select_mode
    
    PRINT *, 'Status:', status
END PROGRAM IfElseBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM IfElseDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    REAL*8 score = 75.5D0
    CHARACTER*20 grade = ""
    
    ! Example 1: Grade assignment with ranges
    IF (score >= 90.0D0) THEN
        grade = "A"
    ELSE IF (score >= 80.0D0) THEN
        grade = "B"
    ELSE IF (score >= 70.0D0) THEN
        grade = "C"
    ELSE IF (score >= 60.0D0) THEN
        grade = "D"
    ELSE
        grade = "F"
    END IF
    
    PRINT *, 'Score:', score, 'Grade:', TRIM(grade)
END PROGRAM IfElseDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM IfElseDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*10 command = "QUIT"
    CHARACTER*40 output = ""
    
    ! Example 1: Command dispatcher
    IF (command == "START") THEN
        output = "System starting"
    ELSE IF (command == "STOP") THEN
        output = "System stopping"
    ELSE IF (command == "QUIT") THEN
        output = "System terminating"
    ELSE
        output = "Unknown command"
    END IF
    
    PRINT *, TRIM(output)
END PROGRAM IfElseDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Multi-Way Decision Logic

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Condition ordering}: Multiple ELSE IF clauses evaluated sequentially, first true executes
@item @strong{String comparison}: Case-sensitive, lexicographic comparison follows ASCII/Unicode ordering
@item @strong{Floating-point ranges}: Use tolerance for range checks with REAL values, never exact equality
@item @strong{Logical operators}: AND, OR in conditions follow short-circuit evaluation rules
@item @strong{Performance}: Early true conditions avoid evaluating remaining branches
@item @strong{Default fallback}: ELSE clause catches all conditions not matched by earlier clauses
@item @strong{Mutation during branch}: Variable modifications in one branch affect subsequent tests
@item @strong{Nested branches}: ELSE IF chains can be deeply nested but reduce code clarity
@item @strong{State changes}: ELSE IF order matters when branch actions have side effects
@end itemize

@subsection Chaining Conditions Effectively

Arrange ELSE IF clauses with most probable conditions first to minimize average branch depth. Keep conditions simple and avoid complex boolean expressions that obscure intent. Use meaningful variable names for conditions rather than buried calculations. Consider extracting complex conditions into boolean functions for reusability.

@node FORTRAN DO Counted Loops
@section DO Counted Loops

The counted DO loop iterates a fixed number of times, controlled by initial, final, and optional step values. Essential for array processing and fixed repetitions with guaranteed termination.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
[LABEL:] DO var = init, limit [, step]
    statements
    [CYCLE]
    [EXIT]
END DO [LABEL]

DO i = 1, 100, 2
    statements
END DO
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{LABEL}: Optional construct label
@item @var{var}: Integer loop variable
@item @var{init}: Starting value (integer)
@item @var{limit}: Ending value (integer)
@item @var{step}: Increment (default 1, nonzero integer)
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM DoBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 i, result = 0, bits = 255
    
    ! Example 1: Simple bit counting loop
    DO i = 0, 7
        IF (IAND(bits, ISHFT(1, i)) /= 0) result = result + 1
    END DO
    
    ! Example 2: Backward counting with negative step
    count_loop: DO i = 31, 0, -1
        IF (IAND(bits, ISHFT(1, i)) /= 0) THEN
            PRINT *, 'Highest bit at position:', i
            EXIT count_loop
        END IF
    END DO count_loop
    
    ! Example 3: Binary pattern generation
    DO i = 1, 8, 2
        PRINT *, 'Odd:', i
    END DO
    
    PRINT *, 'Bit count:', result
END PROGRAM DoBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM DoDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 i, sum_val = 0
    REAL*8 avg = 0.0D0
    
    ! Example 1: Sum calculation loop
    DO i = 1, 100
        sum_val = sum_val + i
    END DO
    
    ! Example 2: Average calculation with REAL
    DO i = 1, 10
        avg = avg + DBLE(i) / 10.0D0
    END DO
    
    ! Example 3: Loop with condition inside
    DO i = 1, 50
        IF (MOD(i, 3) == 0) THEN
            PRINT *, 'Multiple of 3:', i
        END IF
    END DO
    
    PRINT *, 'Sum:', sum_val, 'Average:', avg
END PROGRAM DoDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM DoDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 i
    CHARACTER*30 message
    
    ! Example 1: String repetition loop
    DO i = 1, 5
        PRINT *, 'Iteration', i, 'of 5'
    END DO
    
    ! Example 2: Character generation loop
    DO i = 1, 26
        message = CHAR(64 + i)
        PRINT '(A, I2, A)', message, i, ' - Letter'
    END DO
    
END PROGRAM DoDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Loop Mechanics and Guarantees

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Step must be nonzero}: Zero step causes infinite loop and caught by most modern compilers
@item @strong{Loop variable modification}: Changing loop variable inside loop has undefined behavior
@item @strong{Iteration count}: Calculated once before loop execution: MAX(0, (limit - init + step) / step)
@item @strong{Negative steps}: Loop executes if init >= limit with negative step
@item @strong{Backward iteration}: Negative step enables reverse loops without recalculating indices
@item @strong{Float indices}: Loop variable must be INTEGER, never REAL or other types
@item @strong{Array access}: Loop variable commonly used for array indexing and element processing
@item @strong{Loop exit}: Loop variable retains last value after normal loop completion
@item @strong{Performance}: DO loops typically compile to minimal efficient machine code
@item @strong{Parallelization}: Some DO loops can be parallelized by compilers with appropriate directives
@end itemize

@subsection Loop Optimization Techniques

Modern Fortran compilers aggressively optimize counted loops. Strip-mining and loop unrolling increase register utilization. Array accesses in loops benefit from vectorization on SIMD architectures. Keeping loop bodies small and avoiding function calls improves cache behavior and instruction-level parallelism.

@node FORTRAN DO WHILE
@section DO WHILE Conditional Loop

The DO WHILE construct iterates while a condition remains true. Ideal for input validation, stream processing, and conditions evaluated at loop entry.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
[LABEL:] DO WHILE (logical-condition)
    statements
    [CYCLE]
    [EXIT]
END DO [LABEL]
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{LABEL}: Optional construct label
@item @var{logical-condition}: Evaluated before each iteration
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM DoWhileBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 value = 256, count = 0
    
    ! Example 1: Bit shift loop
    DO WHILE (value > 1)
        value = ISHFT(value, -1)
        count = count + 1
    END DO
    
    ! Example 2: Bit extraction with condition
    value = 511
    binary_loop: DO WHILE (IAND(value, 255) /= 0)
        PRINT *, 'Remaining:', IAND(value, 255)
        value = ISHFT(value, -8)
    END DO binary_loop
    
    PRINT *, 'Bit shifts:', count
END PROGRAM DoWhileBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM DoWhileDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    REAL*8 value = 100.0D0
    INTEGER*4 iterations = 0
    
    ! Example 1: Convergence loop
    DO WHILE (value > 1.0D-6)
        value = value / 2.0D0
        iterations = iterations + 1
    END DO
    
    ! Example 2: Running sum with limit
    REAL*8 sum_val = 0.0D0, increment = 0.1D0
    DO WHILE (sum_val < 10.0D0)
        sum_val = sum_val + increment
    END DO
    
    PRINT *, 'Iterations:', iterations, 'Sum:', sum_val
END PROGRAM DoWhileDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM DoWhileDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*10 status = "RUNNING"
    INTEGER*4 count = 0
    
    ! Example 1: Status-based loop
    DO WHILE (status == "RUNNING")
        count = count + 1
        PRINT *, 'Processing iteration:', count
        IF (count >= 5) status = "STOPPED"
    END DO
    
    PRINT *, 'Final count:', count
END PROGRAM DoWhileDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Conditional Loop Behavior

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Infinite loops}: Condition never becomes false causes infinite loop (programmer must prevent)
@item @strong{Never executes}: If condition false on entry, loop body never runs
@item @strong{Late termination}: Condition checked at loop top only, not during iteration
@item @strong{State-dependent}: Loop execution depends on mutable state, difficult to analyze
@item @strong{Efficiency}: Cannot parallelize due to data-dependent termination condition
@item @strong{Debug complexity}: Infinite loops require debugger or output inspection
@item @strong{Condition evaluation}: Full condition evaluated each iteration, performance depends on complexity
@item @strong{Exit criteria}: Must ensure loop body advances toward exit condition
@item @strong{Resource loops}: Common for I/O operations, resource cleanup, and stream processing
@end itemize

@subsection Using DO WHILE Safely

Ensure loop body modifies state toward exit condition or provides explicit EXIT. Use meaningful variable names for loop conditions. Avoid complex boolean expressions; extract into helper functions for clarity. Add timeout mechanisms for interactive loops to prevent indefinite blocking.

@node FORTRAN DO EXIT
@section DO/EXIT Statement

The EXIT statement terminates loop execution immediately, regardless of loop type or condition. Used for break conditions, error handling, and early termination with clean control flow.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
[LABEL:] DO [WHILE | condition]
    IF (exit-condition) EXIT [label]
    statements
END DO [LABEL]

DO
    IF (condition) EXIT loop_name
    statements
END DO loop_name
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{label}: Loop label for multi-level exit
@item @var{exit-condition}: Boolean triggering exit
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM DoExitBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 i, target = 0, pattern = 15
    
    ! Example 1: Search with exit
    DO i = 0, 31
        IF (IAND(ISHFT(1, i), pattern) /= 0) THEN
            target = i
            EXIT
        END IF
    END DO
    
    ! Example 2: Nested loops with multi-level exit
    outer_loop: DO i = 1, 10
        inner: DO WHILE (i < 20)
            IF (IAND(i, 3) == 0) EXIT outer_loop
            i = i + 1
        END DO inner
    END DO outer_loop
    
    PRINT *, 'Target position:', target
END PROGRAM DoExitBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM DoExitDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    REAL*8 value = 0.0D0, limit = 100.0D0
    INTEGER*4 count = 0
    
    ! Example 1: Loop with limit check
    DO
        value = value + 10.0D0
        count = count + 1
        IF (value > limit) EXIT
    END DO
    
    ! Example 2: Multiple exit conditions
    value = 50.0D0
    search: DO WHILE (value < 1000.0D0)
        IF (value > limit .AND. MOD(INT(value), 7) == 0) EXIT search
        value = value * 1.1D0
    END DO search
    
    PRINT *, 'Final value:', value, 'Count:', count
END PROGRAM DoExitDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM DoExitDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*20 input_line
    CHARACTER*10 command = ""
    INTEGER*4 lines_read = 0
    
    ! Example 1: Input reading loop with termination
    process: DO
        ! Simulated input
        IF (lines_read >= 3) EXIT process
        lines_read = lines_read + 1
        PRINT *, 'Processing line', lines_read
    END DO process
    
    PRINT *, 'Total lines:', lines_read
END PROGRAM DoExitDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Multi-Level Loop Control

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Labeled exit}: Exits to specified loop label, enabling multi-level breaks
@item @strong{Unlabeled exit}: Without label, exits innermost loop only
@item @strong{Late exit}: Processing continues until EXIT statement reached in execution flow
@item @strong{Loop cleanup}: Code after EXIT in same iteration never executes
@item @strong{Nested context}: Each loop level has its own EXIT target
@item @strong{Search termination}: Common for finding elements and early termination
@item @strong{Error recovery}: EXIT enables clean recovery from exceptional conditions
@item @strong{State consistency}: Ensure state remains consistent when exiting early
@item @strong{Performance}: EXIT enables avoiding unnecessary iterations once goal achieved
@item @strong{Debugging}: Label-based exit clarifies which loop terminates in complex nesting
@end itemize

@subsection EXIT with Complex Nesting

Label your loops when nesting multiple levels. This clarifies which loop EXIT targets. Test exit conditions thoroughly to prevent premature or missed exits. Document why early exits occur for code maintainers. Consider extracting multi-level logic into subroutines for clarity.

@node FORTRAN CALL Statement
@section CALL Statement

The CALL statement invokes a subroutine with arguments passed by reference. Enables code reuse, modularization, and side-effect operations on subroutine parameters.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
CALL subroutine-name [([actual-arguments])]

CALL subroutine_name(arg1, arg2, keyword=value, ...)
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{subroutine-name}: Defined subroutine identifier
@item @var{actual-arguments}: Values or variables (passed by reference)
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM CallBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 a = 12, b = 8
    
    CALL swap_binary(a, b)
    PRINT *, 'After swap:', a, b
    
CONTAINS
    SUBROUTINE swap_binary(x, y)
        INTEGER*4, INTENT(INOUT) :: x, y
        INTEGER*4 temp
        temp = x
        x = y
        y = temp
    END SUBROUTINE swap_binary
END PROGRAM CallBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM CallDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    REAL*8 radius = 5.0D0, area = 0.0D0, circumference = 0.0D0
    
    CALL circle_calc(radius, area, circumference)
    PRINT *, 'Area:', area, 'Circumference:', circumference
    
CONTAINS
    SUBROUTINE circle_calc(r, a, c)
        REAL*8, INTENT(IN) :: r
        REAL*8, INTENT(OUT) :: a, c
        REAL*8, PARAMETER :: PI = 3.141592653589793D0
        a = PI * r * r
        c = 2.0D0 * PI * r
    END SUBROUTINE circle_calc
END PROGRAM CallDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM CallDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*30 message = "Initial Message"
    
    CALL modify_string(message)
    PRINT *, TRIM(message)
    
CONTAINS
    SUBROUTINE modify_string(msg)
        CHARACTER*30, INTENT(INOUT) :: msg
        msg = TRIM(msg) // " - Modified"
    END SUBROUTINE modify_string
END PROGRAM CallDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Argument Passing and Type Safety

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Arguments by reference}: Changes in subroutine affect caller's variables permanently
@item @strong{Argument count mismatch}: Causes runtime error or undefined behavior
@item @strong{Intent violations}: Modifying INTENT(IN) parameter causes error at runtime
@item @strong{Array shape mismatch}: Passing incompatible array shapes causes corruption
@item @strong{Type conversion}: Implicit type conversions between argument and parameter
@item @strong{Pointer arguments}: INTENT specification affects aliasing and optimization opportunities
@item @strong{Assumed-shape arrays}: Caller dimension information passed implicitly
@item @strong{Optional arguments}: PRESENT() checks availability before access
@item @strong{Keyword arguments}: Can be passed by name in any order if declared optional
@item @strong{Performance}: Argument passing typically zero-cost due to reference semantics
@end itemize

@subsection Best Practices for CALL Statements

Always specify INTENT(IN), INTENT(OUT), or INTENT(INOUT) for all arguments. This enables compiler optimizations and prevents accidental modifications. Use meaningful subroutine names describing operations. Document complex argument interactions. Consider using derived types to group related arguments, reducing parameter count.

@node FORTRAN FUNCTION Statement
@section FUNCTION Statement

The FUNCTION statement defines a function returning a single value. Functions accept parameters and return results, enabling expression evaluation and computation with clean semantics.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
[type] FUNCTION function-name([parameters]) [RESULT(var)]
    statements
    [function-name = result-value]
    [RETURN]
END FUNCTION [function-name]
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{type}: Return type (REAL, INTEGER, CHARACTER, etc.)
@item @var{function-name}: Function identifier
@item @var{parameters}: Formal parameter list
@item @var{RESULT}: Alternate return variable name
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM FunctionBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 value = 15
    INTEGER*4 bit_count
    
    bit_count = count_set_bits(value)
    PRINT *, 'Set bits:', bit_count
    
CONTAINS
    INTEGER*4 FUNCTION count_set_bits(n)
        INTEGER*4, INTENT(IN) :: n
        INTEGER*4 i, count
        count = 0
        DO i = 0, 31
            IF (IAND(n, ISHFT(1, i)) /= 0) count = count + 1
        END DO
        count_set_bits = count
    END FUNCTION count_set_bits
END PROGRAM FunctionBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM FunctionDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    REAL*8 x = 2.5D0, factorial_result
    
    factorial_result = factorial(INT(x))
    PRINT *, 'Factorial:', factorial_result
    
CONTAINS
    REAL*8 FUNCTION factorial(n)
        INTEGER*4, INTENT(IN) :: n
        INTEGER*4 i
        REAL*8 result
        result = 1.0D0
        DO i = 2, n
            result = result * DBLE(i)
        END DO
        factorial = result
    END FUNCTION factorial
END PROGRAM FunctionDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM FunctionDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*30 str1 = "Hello", str2 = "World"
    CHARACTER*20 combined
    
    combined = concat_strings(str1, str2)
    PRINT *, TRIM(combined)
    
CONTAINS
    CHARACTER*20 FUNCTION concat_strings(s1, s2)
        CHARACTER*20, INTENT(IN) :: s1, s2
        concat_strings = TRIM(s1) // " " // TRIM(s2)
    END FUNCTION concat_strings
END PROGRAM FunctionDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Function Definition and Return Semantics

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{RESULT variable}: Alternate name for return value, cleaner than function name reuse
@item @strong{Function value assignment}: Must assign result before RETURN or END FUNCTION
@item @strong{Type mismatch}: Return value must match declared type or be automatically converted
@item @strong{Early returns}: Multiple RETURN statements allow conditional early exit
@item @strong{No return statement}: Implicit return at END FUNCTION if value assigned
@item @strong{Pure functions}: Functions with no side effects enable aggressive compiler optimizations
@item @strong{Recursive functions}: Functions can call themselves with proper termination conditions
@item @strong{Array returns}: Functions can return arrays of fixed or assumed-shape dimensions
@item @strong{Character return}: STRING functions require length specification in declaration
@item @strong{Performance}: Function call overhead typically negligible on modern CPUs
@end itemize

@subsection Function Design Patterns

Use RESULT variable for clarity, separating input parameters from output variable. Pure functions facilitate parallelization and caching optimizations. Keep function bodies focused on single computations. Return immediately upon completion rather than using intermediate variables. Document expected preconditions and postconditions for function contracts.

@node FORTRAN SUBROUTINE Statement
@section SUBROUTINE Statement

The SUBROUTINE statement defines a subroutine with no inherent return value. Subroutines modify parameters and perform side effects, with explicit control flow handling.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
SUBROUTINE subroutine-name([parameters])
    [IMPLICIT NONE]
    [type declarations]
    statements
    [RETURN]
END SUBROUTINE [subroutine-name]
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{subroutine-name}: Subroutine identifier
@item @var{parameters}: Optional formal parameter list
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM SubroutineBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 flags = 0
    
    CALL set_bits(flags, 3)
    PRINT *, 'Flags after set:', flags
    
CONTAINS
    SUBROUTINE set_bits(f, count)
        INTEGER*4, INTENT(INOUT) :: f
        INTEGER*4, INTENT(IN) :: count
        INTEGER*4 i
        DO i = 0, count-1
            f = IOR(f, ISHFT(1, i))
        END DO
    END SUBROUTINE set_bits
END PROGRAM SubroutineBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM SubroutineDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    REAL*8 arr(3) = [1.0D0, 2.0D0, 3.0D0]
    
    CALL scale_array(arr, 2.5D0)
    PRINT *, arr
    
CONTAINS
    SUBROUTINE scale_array(a, factor)
        REAL*8, INTENT(INOUT) :: a(:)
        REAL*8, INTENT(IN) :: factor
        INTEGER*4 i
        DO i = 1, SIZE(a)
            a(i) = a(i) * factor
        END DO
    END SUBROUTINE scale_array
END PROGRAM SubroutineDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM SubroutineDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*30 message = "Test"
    
    CALL print_bordered(message)
    
CONTAINS
    SUBROUTINE print_bordered(msg)
        CHARACTER*30, INTENT(IN) :: msg
        PRINT *, '=============================='
        PRINT *, TRIM(msg)
        PRINT *, '=============================='
    END SUBROUTINE print_bordered
END PROGRAM SubroutineDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Subroutine Design and Execution

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{No return value}: Subroutines use INTENT(OUT) for results or modify INTENT(INOUT) parameters
@item @strong{Parameter modification}: INTENT(INOUT) for in-place changes, INTENT(OUT) for pure output
@item @strong{Array parameters}: May use assumed-size or assumed-shape for flexible dimensions
@item @strong{Performance}: Subroutine call overhead minimal compared to computation benefits
@item @strong{Side effects}: Subroutines specifically enable side effects and modifications
@item @strong{Implicit execution}: Returns at END SUBROUTINE if no explicit RETURN
@item @strong{Multiple outputs}: INTENT(OUT) parameters accumulate results for caller
@item @strong{State modification}: Can modify COMMON blocks and module variables
@item @strong{Recursion}: Subroutines can call themselves recursively with proper termination
@item @strong{Local variables}: Storage allocated fresh per call, deallocated upon return
@end itemize

@subsection Subroutine Design Methodology

Document parameter roles with INTENT specification. Keep subroutines focused on specific tasks. Limit parameter count through derived types or modules. Test subroutine behavior with various input combinations. Consider error codes or status returns for error handling.

@node FORTRAN RETURN Statement
@section RETURN Statement

The RETURN statement exits a function or subroutine immediately, returning control to the caller. Optional in modern FORTRAN but provides explicit exit points and clarity.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
RETURN [int-label]
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{int-label}: Alternate return label (deprecated, for compatibility)
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM ReturnBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 result
    
    result = binary_search(5)
    PRINT *, 'Result:', result
    
CONTAINS
    INTEGER*4 FUNCTION binary_search(target)
        INTEGER*4, INTENT(IN) :: target
        INTEGER*4 i
        DO i = 0, 31
            IF (IAND(ISHFT(1, i), target) /= 0) THEN
                binary_search = i
                RETURN
            END IF
        END DO
        binary_search = -1
        RETURN
    END FUNCTION binary_search
END PROGRAM ReturnBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM ReturnDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    REAL*8 value
    
    value = safe_divide(10.0D0, 2.0D0)
    PRINT *, 'Result:', value
    
CONTAINS
    REAL*8 FUNCTION safe_divide(a, b)
        REAL*8, INTENT(IN) :: a, b
        IF (ABS(b) < 1.0D-10) THEN
            safe_divide = 0.0D0
            RETURN
        END IF
        safe_divide = a / b
    END FUNCTION safe_divide
END PROGRAM ReturnDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM ReturnDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*20 str = "test"
    
    CALL process_string(str)
    
CONTAINS
    SUBROUTINE process_string(s)
        CHARACTER*20, INTENT(IN) :: s
        IF (LEN_TRIM(s) == 0) THEN
            PRINT *, 'Empty string'
            RETURN
        END IF
        PRINT *, 'String:', TRIM(s)
    END SUBROUTINE process_string
END PROGRAM ReturnDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Control Flow and Return Mechanics

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Early exit}: RETURN before END terminates immediately, any remaining code skipped
@item @strong{Alternate returns}: Deprecated syntax for branch control to numbered labels (RETURN int-label)
@item @strong{Multiple exits}: Code organization with early returns simplifies conditional logic
@item @strong{Stack cleanup}: Automatic cleanup of local variables upon return
@item @strong{State preservation}: Global state and COMMON blocks unchanged by RETURN
@item @strong{Implicit return}: Functions/subroutines return automatically at END if no explicit RETURN
@item @strong{Resource leaks}: Must ensure resource cleanup before RETURN statements
@item @strong{Error codes}: Can assign error codes before RETURN for caller error handling
@item @strong{Debug stepping}: Each RETURN statement represents potential exit point in debugger
@item @strong{Inlining}: Modern compilers may inline RETURN points to eliminate call overhead
@end itemize

@subsection Structured RETURN Usage

Modern programming favors single exit point per function, but early returns improve readability for error conditions. Always clean up resources (files, memory) before any RETURN. Test all return paths through code coverage tools. Use meaningful error codes distinguishing different failure modes.

@node FORTRAN CONTINUE Statement
@section CONTINUE Statement

The CONTINUE statement is a no-operation placeholder, used historically for loop targets and control flow marks. Largely obsolete in modern FORTRAN but present in legacy code.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
CONTINUE

[LABEL:] CONTINUE
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{LABEL}: Optional target label for GOTO statements
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM ContinueBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 i, flags = 0
    
    ! Example 1: Loop with continue marker
    DO i = 0, 3
        IF (IAND(flags, 1) == 0) CYCLE
        CONTINUE
    END DO
    
    ! Example 2: Labeled continue for goto target
    continue_here: CONTINUE
    flags = IOR(flags, 1)
    PRINT *, 'Continued'
    
END PROGRAM ContinueBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM ContinueDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    REAL*8 value = 0.0D0
    INTEGER*4 i
    
    ! Example 1: Continue in loop
    DO i = 1, 5
        IF (MOD(i, 2) == 0) CYCLE
        value = value + DBLE(i)
        CONTINUE
    END DO
    
    PRINT *, 'Sum:', value
END PROGRAM ContinueDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM ContinueDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*20 status = "OK"
    
    ! Example 1: Continue marker
    start: CONTINUE
    PRINT *, 'Status:', TRIM(status)
    
END PROGRAM ContinueDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{No-op statement}: Performs no action
@item @strong{Legacy code}: Common in older FORTRAN 77 programs
@item @strong{Control flow}: Often replaced by DO/EXIT in modern code
@end itemize

@node FORTRAN GOTO Statement
@section GOTO Statement

The GOTO statement performs unconditional jump to labeled statement. Discouraged in modern programming but present for legacy code compatibility and specific use cases.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
GOTO label

[LABEL:] statement
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{label}: Target statement label (numeric, rarely used)
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM GotoBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 i = 5
    
    ! Example 1: Simple goto
    IF (i == 5) GOTO 100
    PRINT *, 'Skipped'
    
100 CONTINUE
    PRINT *, 'Target reached'
    
    ! Example 2: Loop with goto (not recommended)
    i = 0
200 CONTINUE
    IF (i > 3) GOTO 300
    i = i + 1
    GOTO 200
    
300 CONTINUE
    PRINT *, 'Loop completed'
END PROGRAM GotoBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM GotoDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    REAL*8 value = 0.0D0
    
    ! Example 1: Goto for error handling
    value = 5.0D0
    IF (value < 0.0D0) GOTO 400
    PRINT *, 'Valid value:', value
    GOTO 500
    
400 CONTINUE
    PRINT *, 'Error: negative value'
    
500 CONTINUE
    PRINT *, 'Processing complete'
END PROGRAM GotoDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM GotoDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*15 state = "START"
    
    ! Example 1: Simple state transition
    IF (state == "START") GOTO 600
    PRINT *, 'Not started'
    GOTO 700
    
600 CONTINUE
    PRINT *, 'Program started'
    
700 CONTINUE
END PROGRAM GotoDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Unstructured code}: GOTO creates hard-to-follow control flow
@item @strong{Spaghetti code}: Multiple GOTOs create debugging difficulties
@item @strong{Modern alternatives}: Use IF/THEN, loops, or functions instead
@end itemize

@node FORTRAN STOP Statement
@section STOP Statement

The STOP statement terminates program execution immediately with optional exit code. Essential for abnormal termination, error handling, and explicit program completion.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
STOP [stop-code]
STOP [character-literal]
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{stop-code}: Optional integer (0-255) or character string
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM StopBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 flags = 0
    
    ! Example 1: Simple stop
    IF (flags == 0) THEN
        PRINT *, 'Flags not initialized'
        STOP 1
    END IF
    
    ! Example 2: Normal completion
    flags = 1
    PRINT *, 'Processing complete'
    STOP 0
    
END PROGRAM StopBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM StopDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    REAL*8 value = -5.0D0
    
    ! Example 1: Stop on error
    IF (value < 0.0D0) THEN
        PRINT *, 'Error: negative input'
        STOP 'INPUT_ERROR'
    END IF
    
    PRINT *, 'Value:', value
    STOP 0
    
END PROGRAM StopDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM StopDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*30 filename = "input.dat"
    LOGICAL file_exists
    
    ! Example 1: Stop if file missing
    INQUIRE(FILE=filename, EXIST=file_exists)
    IF (.NOT. file_exists) THEN
        PRINT *, 'File not found:', TRIM(filename)
        STOP 'FILE_NOT_FOUND'
    END IF
    
    PRINT *, 'File found'
    STOP 0
    
END PROGRAM StopDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Exit code}: Returned to operating system
@item @strong{No cleanup}: Resources may not flush properly
@item @strong{Character message}: Displayed on termination in some environments
@end itemize

@node FORTRAN PAUSE Statement
@section PAUSE Statement

The PAUSE statement suspends program execution awaiting user input to continue. Useful for interactive programs requiring manual intervention between steps.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
PAUSE [pause-message]
PAUSE [numeric-literal]
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{pause-message}: Optional character literal displayed
@item @var{numeric-literal}: Optional numeric pause code
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM PauseBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 count = 0
    
    ! Example 1: Pause between operations
    count = count + 1
    PRINT *, 'Operation 1 complete. Count:', count
    PAUSE 'Press ENTER to continue...'
    
    count = count + 1
    PRINT *, 'Operation 2 complete. Count:', count
    PAUSE
    
END PROGRAM PauseBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM PauseDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    REAL*8 value = 0.0D0
    
    ! Example 1: Pause for data entry
    PRINT *, 'Enter processing parameters'
    PAUSE 'Ready to process'
    
    value = 42.0D0
    PRINT *, 'Processing value:', value
    
END PROGRAM PauseDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM PauseDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*40 msg = "Calculation Phase 1"
    
    ! Example 1: Display message before pause
    PRINT *, TRIM(msg)
    PAUSE 'Press ENTER for next phase'
    
    msg = "Calculation Phase 2"
    PRINT *, TRIM(msg)
    
END PROGRAM PauseDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Interactive only}: Not suitable for batch processing
@item @strong{Message display}: Some systems ignore PAUSE messages
@item @strong{Deprecated}: Rarely used in modern FORTRAN
@end itemize

@node FORTRAN IMPLICIT Statement
@section IMPLICIT Statement

The IMPLICIT statement sets default type for variables based on initial letter. Controls automatic type assignment and affects entire program scope or named ranges.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
IMPLICIT type [(letter-range [, letter-range]...)]
IMPLICIT NONE

IMPLICIT INTEGER (A-M), REAL (N-Z)
IMPLICIT CHARACTER*20 (A)
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{type}: Default type specification
@item @var{letter-range}: A-Z single letter or A-C range
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM ImplicitBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    ! Forces explicit declaration of all variables
    
    INTEGER*4 ivalue = 42
    REAL*8 dvalue = 3.14D0
    
    PRINT *, ivalue, dvalue
END PROGRAM ImplicitBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM ImplicitDecimal
    IMPLICIT INTEGER (I-N)
    IMPLICIT REAL*8 (A-H, O-Z)
    ! Variables starting with I-N default to INTEGER
    ! Variables starting with A-H, O-Z default to REAL*8
    
    icount = 10
    value = 3.14D0
    result = icount + value
    
    PRINT *, result
END PROGRAM ImplicitDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM ImplicitDisplay
    IMPLICIT CHARACTER*20 (A-Z)
    ! All variables default to CHARACTER*20
    
    message = "Default to character"
    status = "COMPLETE"
    
    PRINT *, TRIM(message)
    PRINT *, TRIM(status)
END PROGRAM ImplicitDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{IMPLICIT NONE}: Best practice, requires explicit declarations
@item @strong{Type scope}: Applies only to following declarations
@item @strong{Default behavior}: Without IMPLICIT, I-N=INTEGER, rest=REAL
@end itemize

@node FORTRAN DIMENSION Statement
@section DIMENSION Statement

The DIMENSION statement declares array variables with specified bounds. Allocates fixed-size arrays and defines array structure at declaration time.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
DIMENSION array(size [, size...]) [, array2...]

DIMENSION iarray(100)
DIMENSION matrix(10, 10), vector(100)
DIMENSION arr1(1:50), arr2(0:99)
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{array}: Array variable name
@item @var{size}: Upper bound (default lower=1) or lower:upper
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM DimensionBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 bits(0:31)
    INTEGER*4 i
    
    ! Example 1: Initialize array
    DO i = 0, 31
        bits(i) = ISHFT(1, i)
    END DO
    
    ! Example 2: Multi-dimensional array
    INTEGER*4 matrix(8, 8)
    matrix(1, 1) = 255
    
    PRINT *, 'Array operations complete'
END PROGRAM DimensionBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM DimensionDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    REAL*8 values(1:1000)
    REAL*8 matrix(10, 10)
    INTEGER*4 i, j
    
    ! Example 1: 1D array initialization
    DO i = 1, 1000
        values(i) = DBLE(i) * 0.1D0
    END DO
    
    ! Example 2: 2D array operations
    DO i = 1, 10
        DO j = 1, 10
            matrix(i, j) = DBLE(i) + DBLE(j)
        END DO
    END DO
    
    PRINT *, 'Dimension operations complete'
END PROGRAM DimensionDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM DimensionDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*30 names(10)
    CHARACTER*50 descriptions(1:5, 1:4)
    
    ! Example 1: String array
    names(1) = "First String"
    
    ! Example 2: 2D string array
    descriptions(1, 1) = "Description 1"
    
    PRINT *, TRIM(names(1))
END PROGRAM DimensionDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Fixed bounds}: Allocated at compile time
@item @strong{Lower bound}: Optional (default 1)
@item @strong{Multi-dimensional}: Stored in row-major order (C-style)
@end itemize

@node FORTRAN PARAMETER Statement
@section PARAMETER Statement

The PARAMETER statement declares compile-time constants with fixed values. Enables named constants with type-safe initialization and prevents accidental modification.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
PARAMETER (name = value [, name = value...])

PARAMETER (PI = 3.141592653589793D0)
PARAMETER (MAX_SIZE = 1000, MIN_SIZE = 1)
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{name}: Constant identifier
@item @var{value}: Compile-time constant expression
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM ParameterBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4, PARAMETER :: MASK = Z'FFFFFFFF'
    INTEGER*4, PARAMETER :: SHIFT = 4
    INTEGER*4 value = 255
    
    ! Example 1: Use binary parameters
    value = IAND(value, MASK)
    value = ISHFT(value, SHIFT)
    
    PRINT *, 'Result:', value
END PROGRAM ParameterBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM ParameterDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    REAL*8, PARAMETER :: PI = 3.141592653589793D0
    REAL*8, PARAMETER :: E = 2.718281828459045D0
    REAL*8, PARAMETER :: RADIUS = 5.0D0
    
    ! Example 1: Calculate area using constant
    REAL*8 area
    area = PI * RADIUS * RADIUS
    
    PRINT *, 'Area:', area
END PROGRAM ParameterDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM ParameterDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*20, PARAMETER :: TITLE = "Program Title"
    CHARACTER*40, PARAMETER :: VERSION = "Version 1.0"
    
    ! Example 1: Display constants
    PRINT *, TRIM(TITLE)
    PRINT *, TRIM(VERSION)
    
END PROGRAM ParameterDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Compile-time only}: Values determined at compilation
@item @strong{Immutable}: Cannot be modified after declaration
@item @strong{Type inference}: Type determined from value if not specified
@end itemize

@node FORTRAN COMMON Statement
@section COMMON Statement

The COMMON statement declares variables in shared global memory blocks. Enables communication between program units and subroutines without explicit parameter passing.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
COMMON [/block-name/] variable [, variable...] &
       [/block-name/] variable [, variable...]

COMMON /GLOBALS/ x, y, z
COMMON x, y, z
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{block-name}: Named COMMON block (optional)
@item @var{variable}: Variables in shared storage
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM CommonBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 flags
    COMMON /FLAGS/ flags
    
    flags = 15
    CALL modify_flags()
    PRINT *, 'Modified flags:', flags
    
CONTAINS
    SUBROUTINE modify_flags()
        IMPLICIT NONE
        INTEGER*4 flags
        COMMON /FLAGS/ flags
        flags = IOR(flags, 128)
    END SUBROUTINE modify_flags
END PROGRAM CommonBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM CommonDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    REAL*8 shared_value
    INTEGER*4 shared_count
    COMMON /DATA/ shared_value, shared_count
    
    shared_value = 42.0D0
    shared_count = 1
    
    CALL process_shared()
    PRINT *, 'Shared:', shared_value, shared_count
    
CONTAINS
    SUBROUTINE process_shared()
        IMPLICIT NONE
        REAL*8 shared_value
        INTEGER*4 shared_count
        COMMON /DATA/ shared_value, shared_count
        shared_value = shared_value * 2.0D0
        shared_count = shared_count + 1
    END SUBROUTINE process_shared
END PROGRAM CommonDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM CommonDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*30 message
    COMMON /OUTPUT/ message
    
    message = "Shared message"
    CALL print_common_message()
    
CONTAINS
    SUBROUTINE print_common_message()
        IMPLICIT NONE
        CHARACTER*30 message
        COMMON /OUTPUT/ message
        PRINT *, TRIM(message)
    END SUBROUTINE print_common_message
END PROGRAM CommonDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Global scope}: Visible to all program units
@item @strong{Memory layout}: Order matters for block compatibility
@item @strong{Deprecated}: Modern code uses modules instead
@end itemize

@node FORTRAN EQUIVALENCE Statement
@section EQUIVALENCE Statement

The EQUIVALENCE statement creates aliases for storage locations, allowing variables to share memory. Enables space-efficient representations and type reinterpretation at runtime.

@subsection Syntax

@smallexample
EQUIVALENCE (name1, name2 [, name3...]) &
           [(name4, name5...)]

EQUIVALENCE (real_val, int_val), (x, y)
@end smallexample

@subsection Parameters

@itemize @bullet
@item @var{name1, name2}: Variables sharing same storage location
@end itemize

@subsection BINARY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM EquivalenceBinary
    IMPLICIT NONE
    INTEGER*4 binary_int
    REAL*4 binary_float
    EQUIVALENCE (binary_int, binary_float)
    
    ! Example 1: Binary reinterpretation
    binary_int = 1065353216  ! IEEE 754 for 1.0
    PRINT *, 'Float representation:', binary_float
    
END PROGRAM EquivalenceBinary
@end smallexample

@subsection DECIMAL Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM EquivalenceDecimal
    IMPLICIT NONE
    REAL*8 value1, value2
    REAL*8 array1(2)
    EQUIVALENCE (value1, array1(1)), (value2, array1(2))
    
    ! Example 1: Array as multiple scalars
    value1 = 10.0D0
    value2 = 20.0D0
    PRINT *, array1
    
END PROGRAM EquivalenceDecimal
@end smallexample

@subsection DISPLAY Examples

@smallexample
PROGRAM EquivalenceDisplay
    IMPLICIT NONE
    CHARACTER*40 full_string
    CHARACTER*10 parts(4)
    EQUIVALENCE (full_string, parts(1))
    
    ! Example 1: String as components
    full_string = "Part1Part2Part3Part4"
    PRINT *, parts(1)
    
END PROGRAM EquivalenceDisplay
@end smallexample

@subsection Edge Cases

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Unsafe}: Can cause undefined behavior if types mismatch
@item @strong{Deprecated}: Modern code uses TRANSFER or ASSOCIATE
@item @strong{Implementation-dependent}: Memory layout varies by system
@end itemize

@c End of FORTRAN comprehensive statements documentation
